# L §5.11 as if: "input[keys] = value: replaces every read of that input at those keys"; "A pin's value is any
# expression ... evaluated first, in the context without that as if (its own pins excluded), then pinned as a
# value. So a pin that reads its own target reads the unpinned value, and there is no cycle."  x = 1:
# x as if {x = x + one} reads the pin's value 1 + 1 = 2.  "The pins of one as if apply only inside it; nested pins
# don't leak to their siblings": (x as if {x = ten}) + x is 10 + 1 = 11.  L §8.2: "A pin given by an expression
# prints its value, pinned: <pin> = v".
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"

input x: int
input one: int
input ten: int

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  x = 1
  one = 1
  ten = 10

ask q1: x as if {x = x + one}
ask q2: (x as if {x = ten}) + x
=== expect
q1: 2
  pinned: x = 2
  cut 2026-12-31
q2: 11
  pinned: x = 10
  cut 2026-12-31
